{
  // Dependency-vulnerability gate for CI (replaces the bare `npm audit --audit-level=high`,
  // which has no way to record a reasoned exception).
  //
  // The gate stays sharp: anything high/critical that is NOT listed below fails the build.
  // `allowlist` is per-advisory (GHSA id), never per-severity — we do not lower the level.
  //
  // Adding an entry requires, in this file: (1) the advisory id, (2) why it does not apply to
  // THIS codebase, measured — not assumed, (3) an expiry date, (4) what removes the need for it.
  // An expired entry is a task, not a renewal: re-check it, do not extend it by reflex.
  //
  // Policy: docs/development/dependency-audit.md
  "$schema": "https://github.com/IBM/audit-ci/raw/main/docs/schema.json",
  "moderate": false,
  "high": true,
  "critical": true,
  "allowlist": [
    // GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2 (react-router, high) was here until 2026-08-14 (#295). It is now
    // REMOVED, not renewed — and the reason it could be removed is NOT the one the entry
    // anticipated, which is worth recording.
    //
    // The entry said "there is NO fix in the 7.x line; the fix is react-router v8 >= 8.2.1, a
    // breaking upgrade", and set its removal condition to the v8 migration wave. That was a
    // correct reading of the advisory on 2026-07-29. On **2026-08-07T18:16:54Z the advisory
    // itself was updated**: the single 7.x-through-8.x range was split in two, and a 7.x fix
    // appeared. Measured from the primary source on 2026-08-14:
    //   gh api /advisories/GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2
    //     { react-router, ">= 7.12.0, < 7.18.2", first_patched 7.18.2 }
    //     { react-router, ">= 8.0.0,  < 8.3.0",  first_patched 8.3.0  }
    // PR #275 put this tree on react-router / react-router-dom **7.18.2**, i.e. outside the
    // range, with no package.json change (caret ^7.9.0 already covered it). `npm audit
    // --audit-level=high` reports 0, and the repository has 0 open Dependabot alerts.
    //
    // The lesson, kept because the next entry will inherit it: this exception was written
    // *well* — the advisory was re-read at authoring time and the "does not apply here"
    // argument was measured against this codebase (no SSR entry, no data-router actions, no
    // RSC). It was still invalidated three days later by a change on the advisory's side, and
    // nothing in this file would have said so. **An expiry date catches an entry that runs
    // out; it does not catch an entry that stops being true.** While an id sits in this list
    // the audit gate is silent about it, so "CI is green" and "we are not exposed" are not
    // the same statement. Re-read the advisory when you touch this file, not only at expiry.
    // GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg (brace-expansion) was here until 2026-08-05 (#277). It is now
    // REMOVED, not renewed: the entry's own stated removal condition — "an upstream 1.x
    // backport" — arrived as **brace-expansion 1.1.18**. `"brace-expansion@1": "^1.1.18"`
    // in overrides takes it, and because that stays inside the 1.x major it keeps the
    // `module.exports = expand` shape `minimatch@3.1.5` calls, so the #261 regression
    // (`TypeError: expand is not a function`) does not come back. Verified on this tree:
    //   node -e "require('.../jsx-a11y/node_modules/minimatch/minimatch.js').braceExpand('{a,b}c')"
    //   → [ 'ac', 'bc' ]
    // and the only two copies are 5.0.9 (hoisted) and 1.1.18 (under eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y),
    // both patched. audit-ci confirms independently: "Consider not allowlisting advisory:
    // GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg."
  ],
}
